          "id": "SP_DINONEMERTES_SHINKAII_KAJIHARA_LINDSAY_2010",
          "name": "Dinonemertes shinkaii Kajihara & Lindsay, 2010",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Dinonemertes",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "External features. The body is broad and flattened (Fig. 1 A). In life, the body is translucent and the internal organs are visible; the brain is pale orange; the stomach, proboscis, lateral nerve cords, and ovaries are white; and the intestine is reddish (Fig. 1 B). On board the R. V. Yokosuka the anterior end of the body around the mouth is somewhat protuberated (Fig. 1 C), while it is not in the natural shape (Fig. 1 A); this might be caused by the distortion of the anterior alimentary canal as a result of the pressure change. The tail is broad, without a distinct fin. After fixation, the intestine became pale orange; the preserved specimen measured 3.3 cm in length, 1.0 cm in maximum width, and 6.0 mm in thickness (Fig. 1 D). Body wall, musculature, and parenchyma. The epidermis was almost completely lost before fixation (Fig. 2 A) except for small areas on the extreme anterior tip of the head (Fig. 2 B), and on the dorsal surface in the posterior region of the body (Fig. 2 C). The connective tissue basement membrane has a deep cup-like structure (Fig. 2 C). The body-wall musculature consists of outer circular and inner longitudinal muscle layers, up to 10 and 30 µm thick, respectively; these are rudimentarily developed on the lateral sides of the body (Fig. 2 A). The presence / absence of the diagonal muscle layer was not ascertained in cross sections. Dorsoventral muscles are not strongly developed (Fig.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:6487433",
          "authorship": "Kajihara & Lindsay",
          "yearPublished": "2010",
          "children": [],
